1. **Limited cognitive demand of T-Maze as primary retention evidence.** While the 1M-step T-Maze result is impressive in scale, the task fundamentally requires retaining a single bit of information (left/right) over a long corridor. A reviewer of the closely related RATE architecture raised a similar concern: "both T-Maze and VizDoom require storing 1 bit of information presented at the initial timestep, for a duration of less than 100 timesteps. So I would not consider this a sufficient comparison between RATE and other related work" (c4w7WVs1z7.md, Reviewer 4). ELMUR extends the corridor length dramatically, but the information-theoretic demand remains minimal. It would strengthen the paper to include more informationally dense long-horizon tasks (requiring recall of multiple cues, structured sequences, or varying information at different time points) to demonstrate that the memory mechanism handles more than trivial retention.

