3. **Few experiment repeats and statistical rigor.** ELMUR reports results with only 3 independent runs. A reviewer of the related SHM paper raised a similar concern: "The experiments have very few repeats (3 seeds) and it is unclear how some baselines were tuned" (We5z3UEnUY.md, Reviewer 4). Given the known high variance in RL experiments and the relatively small number of evaluation episodes (100 per run), 3 seeds may be insufficient for reliable statistical claims, especially on tasks with high variance (e.g., TakeItBack-v0 shows 0.42±0.24 for RATE).

4. **Lack of memory content analysis and visualization.** While the paper provides component ablations, there is no analysis or visualization of what the memory embeddings actually store and retrieve. A reviewer of the related DT-Mem paper similarly noted: "There is no experimental analysis of how the memory module helps improve performance. Please consider ablation studies and visualization to prove that memory is the real contribution (not representation and LoRA tricks)" (FhbZ1PQCaG.md, Reviewer 1). For ELMUR, attention maps over memory slots at different layers and timesteps—showing what information is retained, when it is overwritten, and how the relative bias shapes retrieval—would substantially strengthen the claim that the memory is doing meaningful work beyond what the token track alone provides.

5. **Computational overhead of per-layer memory not thoroughly analyzed.** Each ELMUR layer maintains M memory slots with bidirectional cross-attention (mem2tok + tok2mem), effectively doubling the attention computation per layer. While the paper briefly mentions per-step timing (6.8ms vs 7.2ms for RATE), a reviewer of the NAMM paper raised a similar concern: "the paper lacks information regarding the computational resources needed by the method... It is unclear how (1) the performance fares when varying the cache size and (2) the complexity (runtime and memory) of applying [the method] at inference time" (s1kyHkdTmi.md, Reviewer 2). ELMUR would benefit from a more detailed analysis of how memory size M, number of layers, and MoE FFN contribute to training and inference compute, as well as how performance trades off with M.

6. **Missing important memory-augmented baselines.** The paper compares against DT, RATE, DMamba, BC-MLP, CQL-MLP, and DP, but several closely related memory-augmented transformer architectures discussed in the related work section (Memformer, Block-Recurrent Transformers, RMT, Recurrent Memory Transformer) are not included as experimental baselines. A reviewer of the DT-Mem paper noted: "It is unclear why the proposed memory has advantages over other Transformers with internal/external memory... More baselines need to be included in the experiments... the current baselines have only RMDT as a memory-based Transformer, which is not enough" (FhbZ1PQCaG.md, Reviewer 1). Including at least RMT or Block-Recurrent Transformer as baselines would better contextualize ELMUR's specific design choices (per-layer memory, LRU management, relative bias).

